Stan Kalisch <stan@glyphein.mailforce.net> writes:=0A=
=0A=
>On Fri, Apr 6, 2018, at 3:54 PM, Ion Larranaga Azcue wrote:=0A=
>>=0A=
>> My opinion is that if we are going to have extended error codes, it's=0A=
>> better to use numeric ones and not text based errors.=0A=
>=0A=
>That was my own gut feeling on the least painful way to go, but I'm open t=
o=0A=
>the possibility that gut feeling was woefully naive.=0A=
=0A=
To see why this won't work, you just need to think one step further: Try=0A=
sitting down and defining the numeric error codes you're proposing.=0A=
=0A=
If it's still not obvious: The reason why we need free-form strings is beca=
use=0A=
the numeric codes we already have provide insufficient detail.  To provide =
the=0A=
required detail, you'd need to define numeric codes for every error conditi=
on=0A=
and failed check that every TLS and PKI library (because lots of handshake=
=0A=
failures are due to problems with certs) could wish to communicate.  To tak=
e a=0A=
PKI example, there's "Certificate chain with length %d has a path constrain=
t=0A=
of length %d", which for reasonable values of the two %d leads to, say, aro=
und=0A=
a hundred error codes just for that one condition.  Then take every single=
=0A=
type of error that would need to be communicated and you're into at least 1=
6-=0A=
bit values, or 32- or 64-bit if you take cases like "Packet length of %d=0A=
indicated but only %d bytes were sent".=0A=
=0A=
Even if someone came up with the massive codebook of thousands or tens of=
=0A=
thousands of error codes, which TLS author would want to spend hours paging=
=0A=
through them to find the right code for each situation?=0A=
=0A=
Conversely, if you limit the number of error codes to, say, 5,000, how are =
you=0A=
going to convince TLS library authors to check for and report only those=0A=
errors?  What happens if new error types are defined?=0A=
=0A=
Peter.=

